To resolve the "Ghost Completion" (Silent Async Failure) bug, we need to ensure that the batch creation process is "All or Nothing."
In the previous version, if one parallel thread failed, the join() would throw an exception, but other threads might have already committed thousands of rows to Oracle. This left the database in a "zombie" state—rows existed, but the Master record said "ERROR."
Here is the complete, production-ready executeAsyncBatchCreation method. It includes the Lock Fix (from Bug #1) and the Transaction/Cleanup Fix (for Bug #2).
JournalRequestServiceImpl.java (Updated executeAsyncBatchCreation)
/**
 * Rule 3: Fixed executeAsyncBatchCreation logic.
 * addressing Bug #2 (Ghost Completion): 
 * Ensures that if any parallel chunk fails, a robust cleanup is triggered 
 * to remove "zombie" rows from Oracle, maintaining data integrity.
 */
@Override
@Async("bulkExecutor")
@Transactional
public void executeAsyncBatchCreation(String batchId, String requestId, String commonRemarks, String creatorId, Integer creatorRole) {
    String lockKey = "LOCK_REQ_" + requestId;
    MDC.put("batchId", batchId);
    MDC.put("user", creatorId);

    log.info("ASYNC_BATCH_START | Batch: {} | Source: {}", batchId, requestId);
    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

    try {
        progressService.sendProgress(batchId, creatorId, 5, "PROCESSING", "Preparing Data...");

        List<JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData> cachedRows = journalBulkValidationService.getValidRowsFromCache(requestId);
        if (cachedRows == null || cachedRows.isEmpty()) {
            logAudit(creatorId, "CREATE_FAIL", "BATCH_ASYNC", "Cache Expired for " + batchId);
            progressService.sendProgress(batchId, creatorId, 0, "FAILED", "Data cache expired or empty.");
            return;
        }

        int totalRows = cachedRows.size();
        int batchSize = 5000; 

        List<CompletableFuture<Void>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        LongAdder totalDebit = new LongAdder();
        LongAdder totalCredit = new LongAdder();
        AtomicInteger completedChunks = new AtomicInteger(0);
        int totalChunks = (int) Math.ceil((double) totalRows / batchSize);

        for (int i = 0; i < totalRows; i += batchSize) {
            final int startIdx = i;
            final int endIdx = Math.min(i + batchSize, totalRows);
            final List<JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData> chunk = cachedRows.subList(startIdx, endIdx);

            CompletableFuture<Void> future = CompletableFuture.runAsync(() ->
                            processChunkRaw(chunk, batchId, commonRemarks, creatorId, creatorRole, startIdx, totalDebit, totalCredit), bulkExecutor)
                    .thenRun(() -> {
                        int done = completedChunks.incrementAndGet();
                        int percent = 5 + (done * 90 / totalChunks);
                        progressService.sendProgress(batchId, creatorId, percent, "PROCESSING",
                                String.format("Created %d/%d records...", Math.min(done * batchSize, totalRows), totalRows));
                    });
            futures.add(future);
        }

        /**
         * Rule 1 & Bug Fix #2: Use allOf().join() but catch CompletionException specifically.
         * If any thread fails, we MUST trigger the cleanup logic to avoid zombie rows.
         */
        try {
            CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])).join();
        } catch (CompletionException ce) {
            // Unwrap to find the actual SQL or logic cause
            Throwable cause = ce.getCause();
            log.error("ASYNC_CHUNK_FAILURE | Batch: {} | Reason: {}", batchId, 
                      (cause != null ? cause.getClass().getSimpleName() : "Unknown"));
            throw new CustomExceptions.BatchProcessingException("One or more data chunks failed to persist.", cause);
        }

        // Insert Master Record only after ALL chunks are confirmed successful
        Timestamp ts = Timestamp.valueOf(LocalDateTime.now());
        String sumSql = "INSERT INTO JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER (BATCH_ID, CREATOR_ID, REQ_DATE, BATCH_REMARKS, TOTAL_ROWS, TOTAL_DEBIT, TOTAL_CREDIT, BATCH_STATUS) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)";
        jdbcTemplate.update(sumSql, batchId, creatorId, ts, commonRemarks, totalRows,
                BigDecimal.valueOf(totalDebit.sum()), BigDecimal.valueOf(totalCredit.sum()), "PENDING");

        logAudit(creatorId, "CREATE_SUCCESS", "BATCH_ASYNC", "Successfully created batch " + batchId);
        progressService.sendProgress(batchId, creatorId, 100, "COMPLETED", "Batch Created Successfully");
        sendNotification(creatorId, null, "Batch " + batchId + " created successfully.", batchId);

        NotificationConfigDto config = permissionConfigService.getConfig("JOURNAL_AUTH");
        if (config != null) {
            String msg = String.format("New Batch %s by %s requires approval.", batchId, creatorId);
            sendNotification(creatorId, config.getTargetRoles(), msg, batchId);
        }

        log.info("BATCH_CREATION_FINISHED | Batch: {} | TotalRows: {} | Duration: {}ms", batchId, totalRows, System.currentTimeMillis() - start);

    } catch (CustomExceptions.BatchProcessingException | DataAccessException e) {
        // Rule 1: Handling specific batch failures
        log.error("BATCH_FATAL_ERROR | Batch: {} | Status: Triggering Cleanup", batchId);
        handleAsyncFailure(batchId, creatorId, requestId, "Database persistence failed. Partial data has been purged.");
    } catch (Exception e) {
        // Rule 1 & 2: Final safety net for unexpected crashes
        log.error("UNEXPECTED_SYSTEM_FAULT | Batch: {} | Type: {}", batchId, e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        handleAsyncFailure(batchId, creatorId, requestId, "An internal error occurred. Please retry.");
    } finally {
        // Bug Fix #1: Cleanup Redis state in the finally block to ensure no locked Request IDs
        redisTemplate.delete(lockKey);
        redisTemplate.delete("DATA_" + requestId);
        MDC.clear();
    }
}

Critical Change: handleAsyncFailure Enhancement
To properly fix Bug #2, ensure your cleanupFailedBatch (called by handleAsyncFailure) uses the Primary Oracle DataSource to wipe out any rows that were committed by the successful chunks before the failure happened.
private void handleAsyncFailure(String batchId, String creatorId, String requestId, String message) {
    // 1. Wipe out any zombie rows from the JOURNAL_REQUEST table for this batch
    cleanupFailedBatch(batchId);
    
    // 2. Update status so the user sees the specific error
    jdbcTemplate.update("UPDATE JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER SET BATCH_STATUS = 'ERROR', EXECUTOR_REMARKS = ? WHERE BATCH_ID = ?", 
                        message, batchId);
    
    progressService.sendProgress(batchId, creatorId, 0, "FAILED", message);
}

How this fixes "Ghost Completion":
 * Strict Unwrapping: By catching CompletionException, the code identifies immediately if any parallel thread crashed.
 * Atomic-like Behavior: Since we now catch the failure at the join() point, we can call cleanupFailedBatch to delete the "zombie" rows left behind by the threads that did succeed.
 * No more stuck locks: Moving redisTemplate.delete to finally ensures that even if the whole JVM stutters, the user isn't locked out for 60 minutes.
